|~ `::qlang/any`, the kind beneath every kind: the verbs of any value, and the contracts of the verbs that
   several kinds answer, each residing on `::qlang/any`, the kind beneath
   every kind. A contract is a verb without a body: its page and its
   laws hold for every verb of its name, what a residence
   leaves out it says, and a call whose subject no residence serves
   reaches it and is refused with `:addresses`, the residences of the
   name. ~|

::qlang/any
  |~~ The kind beneath every kind, where the verbs every value answers
      live: `type`, `eq`, `doc`, `print`, `explain`, `elide` and
      their kin. A verb that means the same for any value is written once
      here, and the walk from a name to its verb ends here, after the
      kinds of the subject. No value answers it as its `type`, since each
      has a kind of its own above it.
      ::snippet~(::qlang/any | spec | /verbs) lists the verbs of every value.
      ::link~(::qlang | doc :verbs)

      ~(::qlang/any | spec | /verbs | has ::any/type)
      ~(5 | type | eq ::qlang/any | not)
  ~~|

::ToPlainUnencodableValueError
  |~~ JSON has no form for a value a host installed that is none of the
      kinds of qlang; a function a host bound is written as
      `<host-fn name>`, and any other such value is refused, so the query
      projects around it.
  ~~|

::SourceLoadError
  |~~ The platform resolved a `#qlang/<ns>` logical name to a path
      or URL the host could not read. The descriptor carries
      `:host` (`:node` / `:web`), `:logicalName`, `:sourceLocation`,
      and either the underlying `:cause` or the HTTP `:status`. A
      host that ships its own catalog answers the `:qlang/locator`
      contract and never reaches the platform loader.
  ~~|

:gt
  |~~ Whether the subject follows the value the call gives in the one
      order of their kind. Numbers, strings, keywords and tag names each
      compare with a value of their own kind, a residence on each of
      them checking both; a subject of another kind reaches this
      contract.

      ~(10 | gt 5 | eq true)
      ~("b" | gt "a" | eq true)
      ~(null | gt 1 !| /addresses | has ::number/gt)
  ~~|
  ::verb~()

:gte
  |~~ Whether the subject follows or equals the value the call gives in
      the one order of their kind, which `gt` states for every kind it
      compares.

      ~(10 | gte 10 | eq true)
      ~(:b | gte :a | eq true)
  ~~|
  ::verb~()

:lt
  |~~ Whether the subject precedes the value the call gives in the one
      order of their kind, which `gt` states for every kind it compares.

      ~(5 | lt 10 | eq true)
      ~("a" | lt "b" | eq true)
  ~~|
  ::verb~()

:lte
  |~~ Whether the subject precedes or equals the value the call gives in
      the one order of their kind, which `gt` states for every kind it
      compares.

      ~(10 | lte 10 | eq true)
      ~(::a | lte ::b | eq true)
  ~~|
  ::verb~()

:count
  |~~ The number of elements a container holds: a vector's, a set's,
      and a map's entries; and the number of characters of a string.

      ~([1 2 3] | count | eq 3)
      ~(#[:a :b] | count | eq 2)
      ~({:x 1 :y 2 :z 3} | count | eq 3)
      ~("abc" | count | eq 3)
      ~(42 | count !| /addresses | has ::vec/count)
  ~~|
  ::verb~()

:empty
  |~~ Whether a container holds no element.

      ~([] | empty | eq true)
      ~([1] | empty | eq false)
      ~({} | empty | eq true)
  ~~|
  ::verb~()

:first
  |~~ The first element of a vector, the least of a set, or the first
      value of a map, and null for an empty container.

      ~([10 20 30] | first | eq 10)
      ~(#[:c :a :b] | first | eq :a)
      ~({:a 1 :b 2} | first | eq 1)
      ~([] | first | eq null)
  ~~|
  ::verb~()

:last
  |~~ The last element of a vector, the greatest of a set, or the last
      value of a map, and null for an empty container.

      ~([10 20 30] | last | eq 30)
      ~(#[:c :a :b] | last | eq :c)
      ~({:a 1 :b 2} | last | eq 2)
      ~([] | last | eq null)
  ~~|
  ::verb~()

:sum
  |~~ The sum of the elements of a vector or a set, or of the values of a
      map, each a number; an empty container sums to 0.

      ~([1 2 3 4] | sum | eq 10)
      ~([] | sum | eq 0)
      ~(#[1 2 3] | sum | eq 6)
      ~({:a 10 :b 20} | sum | eq 30)
  ~~|
  ::verb~()

::SumElementNotNumberError
  |~~ `sum` adds numbers; `:index` names the first element that is
      none, and a field is read first, `* /amount | sum`.
      ~(
        [1 "two" 3] | sum
        !| [type /actualType /index]
        |  eq [::SumElementNotNumberError ::string 1]
       )
  ~~|

::SumResultNotFiniteError
  |~~ Every element is of the right kind, and the running
      total still answered outside the finite-double domain. The
      total is read at each element in the subject's order, so a subject
      whose partial sums leave the domain lifts here while its
      mathematical total sits inside it — `:index` names the element
      the total crossed at, and the subject splits there.

      ~(
        [1e308 1e308] | sum
        !| [type /index]
        |  eq [::SumResultNotFiniteError 1]
       )
  ~~|

:min
  |~~ The first element in the one order of values, the order `sort`
      answers, of a vector, a set, or the values of a map, or the element
      whose key answers the first value, the key read as `sort` reads it;
      the element itself is the answer, the first of equal ones, and an
      empty container yields null.

      ~([3 1 4 1 5] | min | eq 1)
      ~({:a 3 :b 1} | min | eq 1)
      ~([3 "a" null] | min | eq null)
      ~(#[3 1 4] | min | eq 1)
      ~([{:age 30} {:age 20}] | min ~(/age) | eq {:age 20})
  ~~|
  ::verb~()

:max
  |~~ The last element in the one order of values, the order `sort`
      answers, of a vector, a set, or the values of a map, or the element
      whose key answers the last value, the key read as `sort` reads it;
      the element itself is the answer, the first of equal ones, and an
      empty container yields null.

      ~([3 1 4 1 5] | max | eq 5)
      ~({:a 3 :b 1} | max | eq 3)
      ~([3 "a" null] | max | eq "a")
      ~(#[3 1 4] | max | eq 4)
      ~([{:age 30} {:age 40}] | max ~(/age) | eq {:age 40})
      ~({:a 3 :b 1} | entries | max ~(/1) | /0 | eq :a)
  ~~|
  ::verb~()

:filter
  |~~ Keeps the elements of a container a predicate accepts, in a
      container of the same kind, the way a query narrows what it found
      before it reads it.

      The predicate answers a boolean, and what it answers true for stays:
      ~([1 2 3 4 5] | filter ~(gt 2) | eq [3 4 5])
      The predicate reads each element as its subject:
      ~([{:age 25} {:age 15}] | filter ~(/age | gte 18) | eq [{:age 25}])
      A verb stands where a quote would:
      ~([1 -2 3] | :positive ::verb~(gt 0) | filter ~positive | eq [1 3])
      ~(#[1 -2 3] | filter ::verb~(gt 0) | eq #[1 3])
      A map keeps the entries whose values the predicate accepts:
      ~({:a 1 :b 2 :c 3} | filter ~(gt 1) | eq {:b 2 :c 3})
      ~({} | filter ~(gt 0) | eq {})
  ~~|
  ::verb~()

::FilterConditionNotBooleanError
  |~~ The predicate of `filter` answers a boolean for each element;
      `:index` names the element whose predicate answered another kind.
      ~(
        [1 2] | filter ~(1)
        !| [type /index /actualType]
        |  eq [::FilterConditionNotBooleanError 0 ::number]
       )
  ~~|

::FilterPredicateNotQuoteError
  |~~ The predicate of `filter` is code it runs on each element, so it takes a
      quote, `filter ~(gt 1)`; a value computed at the call is refused.
      ~(
        [1 2 3] | filter (count | gt 1)
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::FilterPredicateNotQuoteError ::boolean]
       )
      A step written without its quote runs on the whole subject at the
      call, and the error it answers is refused alike:
      ~(
        [{:a true}] | filter /a
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::FilterPredicateNotQuoteError ::ProjectionSequenceKeyNotIntegerError]
       )
  ~~|

:every
  |~~ Whether a predicate holds for every element of a container, a
      map's elements being its values: the question a law asks of a
      listing, `every ~(…)`. It stops at the first false.

      True when every element passes, false at the first that fails:
      ~([2 4 6 8] | every ~(gt 0) | eq true)
      ~([1 2 3] | every ~(gt 2) | eq false)
      A verb stands where a quote would:
      ~([2 4 6] | :positive ::verb~(gt 0) | every ~positive | eq true)
      ~({:a 1 :b 2} | every ::verb~(gt 0) | eq true)
      An empty container answers true:
      ~([] | every ~(gt 0) | eq true)
      A map is asked of its values:
      ~({:a 1 :b -2 :c 3} | every ~(gt 0) | eq false)
  ~~|
  ::verb~()

::EveryConditionNotBooleanError
  |~~ The predicate of `every` answers a boolean for each element;
      `:index` names the element whose predicate answered another kind.
      ~(
        [1 2] | every ~(1)
        !| [type /index /actualType]
        |  eq [::EveryConditionNotBooleanError 0 ::number]
       )
  ~~|

::EveryPredicateNotQuoteError
  |~~ The predicate of `every` is code it runs on each element, so it takes a
      quote, `every ~(gt 0)`; a value computed at the call is refused.
      ~(
        [1 2 3] | every (count | gt 1)
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::EveryPredicateNotQuoteError ::boolean]
       )
  ~~|

:any
  |~~ Whether a predicate holds for some element of a container, a map's
      elements being its values. It stops at the first true.

      True at the first element that passes, false when none does:
      ~([1 2 3] | any ~(gt 2) | eq true)
      ~([1 2 3] | any ~(gt 99) | eq false)
      A verb stands where a quote would:
      ~([1 2 3] | :big ::verb~(gt 2) | any ~big | eq true)
      ~({:a -1 :b 2} | any ::verb~(gt 0) | eq true)
      An empty container answers false:
      ~([] | any ~(gt 0) | eq false)
      A map is asked of its values:
      ~({:a -1 :b 0 :c 2} | any ~(gt 0) | eq true)
  ~~|
  ::verb~()

::AnyConditionNotBooleanError
  |~~ The predicate of `any` answers a boolean for each element;
      `:index` names the element whose predicate answered another kind.
      ~(
        [1 2] | any ~(1)
        !| [type /index /actualType]
        |  eq [::AnyConditionNotBooleanError 0 ::number]
       )
  ~~|

::AnyPredicateNotQuoteError
  |~~ The predicate of `any` is code it runs on each element, so it takes a
      quote, `any ~(gt 2)`; a value computed at the call is refused.
      ~(
        [1 2 3] | any (count | gt 1)
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::AnyPredicateNotQuoteError ::boolean]
       )
  ~~|

:groupBy
  |~~ Partitions a vector or a set into a map keyed by the keyword the key
      answers for each element, a string or a number filed under the
      keyword of its text, so a field of JSON groups as it is: a vector's
      buckets are vectors and a set's are sets. The entries follow the
      first occurrence of each key, and each bucket the subject's order.

      ~([{:dept :eng :name "a"} {:dept :sales :name "b"} {:dept :eng :name "c"}] | groupBy ~(/dept) | /eng * /name | eq ["a" "c"])
      A string or a number keys its bucket by the keyword of its text:
      ~([{:s "paid"} {:s "open"} {:s "paid"}] | groupBy ~(/s) * count | eq {:paid 2 :open 1})
      ~([{:k 1} {:k 2} {:k 1}] | groupBy ~(/k) | keys | eq #[:"1" :"2"])
      Without a key an element files under itself:
      ~(["b" "a" "b"] | groupBy * count | eq {:b 2 :a 1})
  ~~|
  ::verb~()

::GroupByKeyNotKeywordError
  |~~ The key `groupBy` files an element under is a keyword, or a string
      or a number, filed under the keyword of its text, since the keys of
      a map are keywords; any other value is refused, and `:index` names
      the element.
      ~(
        [{:k true} {:k 2}] | groupBy ~(/k)
        !| [type /actualType /index]
        |  eq [::GroupByKeyNotKeywordError ::boolean 0]
       )
  ~~|

::GroupByKeyNotQuoteError
  |~~ The key of `groupBy` is code it runs on each element, so it takes a
      quote, `groupBy ~(/kind)`; a value computed at the call is refused.
      ~(
        [{:kind :a}] | groupBy :a
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::GroupByKeyNotQuoteError ::keyword]
       )
  ~~|

:indexBy
  |~~ Collapses a vector or a set into a map keyed by the keyword the key
      answers for each element, a string or a number filed under the
      keyword of its text; of two elements the key answers alike, the
      last wins. Without a key an element files under itself.

      ~([{:id :a :name "alice"} {:id :b :name "bob"}] | indexBy ~(/id) | /a/name | eq "alice")
      ~([{:id 7 :name "ann"}] | indexBy ~(/id) | /7/name | eq "ann")
      ~([:x :y] | indexBy | eq {:x :x :y :y})
  ~~|
  ::verb~()

::IndexByKeyNotKeywordError
  |~~ The key `indexBy` files an element under is a keyword, or a string
      or a number, filed under the keyword of its text, since the keys of
      a map are keywords; any other value is refused, and `:index` names
      the element.
      ~(
        [{:k [1]}] | indexBy ~(/k)
        !| [type /actualType /index]
        |  eq [::IndexByKeyNotKeywordError ::vec 0]
       )
  ~~|

::IndexByKeyNotQuoteError
  |~~ The key of `indexBy` is code it runs on each element, so it takes a
      quote, `indexBy ~(/id)`; a value computed at the call is refused.
      ~(
        [{:id :a}] | indexBy :a
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::IndexByKeyNotQuoteError ::keyword]
       )
  ~~|

:sort
  |~~ The elements in the one order of values, the order every value of
      the language takes against every other, so a listing can be ranked
      before it is read; `sort ~(key)` ranks by the value the key answers
      for each element. The key answers what to compare, and the one order
      compares it: a vector it answers ranks element by element, the first
      place first and each later one among the elements the places before
      it leave alike, and `desc` turns a place to descend, so
      `sort ~([(/count | desc) /name])` reads as `ORDER BY count DESC,
      name`. Elements the key leaves alike keep the order of the subject.

      Numbers ascend by value, equal ones side by side:
      ~([3 1 4 1 5] | sort | eq [1 1 3 4 5])
      Kinds come first, null, boolean, number, string, keyword, tag name,
      vector, set, map, quote, doc, error, elision and desc, then every
      other tag by its name:
      ~([3 null "x" 1] | sort | eq [null 1 3 "x"])
      Keywords and tag names order by their names, and a set sorts into a
      vector, since the order is imposed and a set keeps its own:
      ~(#[:y :x :z] | sort | eq [:x :y :z])
      ~([::B ::A] | sort | eq [::A ::B])
      A vector key ranks by its first place, then by the next among the
      elements the first leaves alike:
      ~([{:a 1 :b 2} {:a 1 :b 1} {:a 0 :b 9}] | sort ~([/a /b]) * /b | eq [9 1 2])
      ~([3 null 1] | sort ~([(eq null) /]) | eq [1 3 null])
      `desc` turns the key, or one place of it, to descend:
      ~([{:k 1} {:k 2}] | sort ~(/k | desc) | eq [{:k 2} {:k 1}])
      ~([{:n 1 :s "b"} {:n 2 :s "c"} {:n 2 :s "a"}] | sort ~([(/n | desc) /s]) * /s | eq ["a" "c" "b"])
      A key that answers an error answers the call, a place of a vector it
      answers among them, so a key that misses its field is refused where
      it is read:
      ~([{:a 2} {:b 1}] | sort ~(/a) !| type | eq ::ProjectionKeyNotInMapError)
      ~([{:a 2} {:b 1}] | sort ~([/a]) !| type | eq ::ProjectionKeyNotInMapError)
      A map sorts its entries by their values and keeps the keys:
      ~({:a 3 :b 1 :c 2} | sort | vals | eq [1 2 3])
  ~~|
  ::verb~()

::SortKeyNotQuoteError
  |~~ The key of `sort` is code it runs on each element, so it takes a
      quote, `sort ~(/age)`; a value computed at the call is refused.
      ~(
        [3 1 2] | sort 1
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::SortKeyNotQuoteError ::number]
       )
  ~~|

:desc
  |~~ The subject turned to descend, a value of `::desc`: a key of
      `sort`, `min` or `max`, or a place of a vector key, that ranks the
      greater first, whatever the kind of what it ranks.

      ~([1 3 2] | sort ~(desc) | eq [3 2 1])
      ~([{:n 1} {:n 2}] | max ~(/n | desc) | eq {:n 1})
      ~(5 | desc | type | eq ::desc)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:returns ::desc | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/desc})


:take
  |~~ The first count elements in the subject's order, a set's least
      among them, a map's first count entries and a string's first count
      characters, in a value of the subject's kind. The count is a whole number: past the length the
      whole container comes back, and a negative count takes nothing.

      ~([1 2 3 4 5] | take 3 | eq [1 2 3])
      ~(#[:d :c :b :a] | take 2 | eq #[:a :b])
      ~([1 2 3] | take -1 | eq [])
      ~("db_host" | take 2 | eq "db")
  ~~|
  ::verb~()

::TakeCountNotIntegerError
  |~~ The count of `take` must be a whole number — a fractional count
      cannot name a prefix length.
      ~(
        [1 2 3] | take 0.5
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::TakeCountNotIntegerError ::number]
       )
  ~~|

:drop
  |~~ The container with its first count elements removed, a map's first
      count entries and a string's first count characters, in a value of
      the subject's kind. The count is
      a whole number: past the length the empty container comes back,
      and a negative count drops nothing.

      ~([1 2 3 4 5] | drop 2 | eq [3 4 5])
      ~(#[:d :c :b :a] | drop 2 | eq #[:c :d])
      ~([1 2 3] | drop -1 | eq [1 2 3])
      ~("db_host" | drop 3 | eq "host")
  ~~|
  ::verb~()

::DropCountNotIntegerError
  |~~ The count of `drop` must be a whole number — a fractional count
      cannot name how many to remove.
      ~(
        [1 2 3] | drop 0.5
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::DropCountNotIntegerError ::number]
       )
  ~~|

:at
  |~~ Indexed access: the element of a vector or a set at an integer
      index, a set indexing in its one order and a negative index
      counting from the end, or the value of a map at a key; a miss
      answers null, the soft counterpart of the strict `/key`
      projection.

      ~([10 20 30] | at 1 | eq 20)
      ~([10 20 30] | at 99 | eq null)
      ~(#[:a :b :c] | at -1 | eq :c)
      ~({:a 1 :b 2} | at :a | eq 1)
  ~~|
  ::verb~()

::AtIndexNotIntegerError
  |~~ The index of `at` on a vector or a set must be a whole number.
      ~(
        [1 2 3] | at 0.5
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::AtIndexNotIntegerError ::number]
       )
  ~~|

:distinct
  |~~ The constructor of the set: the elements of a vector in the one
      order of values, each once, so a downstream verb receives a
      value whose kind says «no duplicates»; a set passes through as it
      is. Duplication is decided by structural equality, the axiom of
      `eq`, so two maps, vectors or sets of the same content collapse;
      `#[…]` and `::set[…]` mint the same value.

      ~([2 1 2 3 1] | distinct | eq #[1 2 3])
      ~([2 1 2 3 1] | distinct | payload | eq [1 2 3])
      ~([{:id 1} {:id 2} {:id 1}] | distinct | eq #[{:id 1} {:id 2}])
      ~(#[1 2 3] | distinct | eq #[1 2 3])
  ~~|
  ::verb~()

:reverse
  |~~ The container in reverse order: a vector and a set reverse into a
      vector, since a set keeps its own order, and a map reverses its
      entries and keeps the keys.

      ~([1 2 3] | reverse | eq [3 2 1])
      ~(#[:a :b :c] | reverse | eq [:c :b :a])
      ~({:a 1 :b 2} | reverse | vals | eq [2 1])
  ~~|
  ::verb~()

:flat
  |~~ One level of nesting flattened: the vectors and sets among the
      elements are spliced in, the other elements pass through, and the
      answer is of the subject's kind, so a set of sets flattens into
      their union.

      ~([[1 2] [3] [4 5]] | flat | eq [1 2 3 4 5])
      ~(#[#[1 2] #[2 3]] | flat | eq #[1 2 3])
  ~~|
  ::verb~()

:reduce
  |~~ The universal left fold, which threads an accumulator across the
      elements of a vector, a set in its order, or the values of a map,
      and collapses them to one value; `sum`, `count`, `max` and the
      builders of structures all factor through it. The reducer is
      applied as `reducer accumulator element`.

      An operand of two places folds through its bound form, `acc | add
      element`:
      ~([1 2 3 4 5] | reduce 0 ~(add) | eq 15)
      ~([1 2 3 4 5] | reduce 1 ~(mul) | eq 120)
      ~(["a" "b" "c"] | reduce "" ~(append) | eq "abc")
      ~([#[1] #[2 3]] | reduce #[] ~(union) | eq #[1 2 3])
      A verb takes the accumulator as its subject and the element in its
      first slot:
      ~(:max2 ::verb~(:x ::number | if (gt x) ~(/) ~(x)) | [3 1 4 1 5] | reduce 0 ~max2 | eq 5)
      ~(:tally ::verb~(:_ ::any | add 1) | [10 20 30] | reduce 0 ~tally | eq 3)
      The seed is the first accumulator, answered as it is for an empty
      subject:
      ~([] | reduce 42 ~(add) | eq 42)
  ~~|
  ::verb~()

::ReduceReducerNotBinaryError
  |~~ The quote of `reduce` names one verb, which the fold calls with the
      accumulator as its subject and the element in its first slot,
      `reduce 0 ~(add)`; a quote of several steps, a literal or a name of
      no verb is refused. A fold of several steps is a verb
      declared first, whose slot names the element.
      ~(
        [1 2 3] | reduce 0 ~(mul 2 | add)
        !| type
        |  eq ::ReduceReducerNotBinaryError
       )
      ~(
        :step ::verb~(:el ::number | mul 2 | add el)
        | [1 2 3] | reduce 0 ~(step)
        | eq 11
       )
  ~~|

::ReduceReducerNotQuoteError
  |~~ The reducer of `reduce` is code it runs on the accumulator with each element, so it takes a
      quote, `reduce 0 ~(add)`; a value computed at the call is refused.
      ~(
        [1 2 3] | reduce 0 1
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::ReduceReducerNotQuoteError ::number]
       )
  ~~|

:union
  |~~ The union of two operands of one kind: two sets merged in the one
      order, or two maps merged with the second one's value winning a
      key both hold; over a vector of operands it folds them. The bound
      form `a | union b` reads b against a, and the full form `union a
      b` reads both against the value in the pipe.

      ~(#[:a :b] | union #[:b :c] | eq #[:a :b :c])
      ~({:a 1} | union {:b 2} | eq {:a 1 :b 2})
      ~([#[:a :b] #[:b :c]] | union | eq #[:a :b :c])
  ~~|
  ::verb~()

:minus
  |~~ The first operand without what the second holds: a set without the
      elements of another, a map without the keys of a map, or of a vector
      or a set of keywords or strings; over a vector of operands it folds
      them.

      ~(#[:a :b :c] | minus #[:b] | eq #[:a :c])
      ~({:a 1 :b 2 :tmp 3} | minus #[:tmp] | eq {:a 1 :b 2})
      ~([#[:a :b :c] #[:b]] | minus | eq #[:a :c])
  ~~|
  ::verb~()

:inter
  |~~ What the first operand and the second both hold: the elements two
      sets share, or the entries of a map whose keys a map, or a vector or
      a set of keywords or strings, holds; over a vector of operands it
      folds them.

      ~(#[:a :b :c] | inter #[:b :d] | eq #[:b])
      ~({:a 1 :b 2 :c 3} | inter #[:a :b] | eq {:a 1 :b 2})
      ~([#[:a :b :c] #[:b :d]] | inter | eq #[:b])
  ~~|
  ::verb~()

:has
  |~~ Whether a map holds a key or a set holds an element.

      ~({:name "Alice"} | has :name | eq true)
      ~(#[:a :b :c] | has :b | eq true)
      ~(42 | has :k !| /addresses | has ::map/has)
  ~~|
  ::verb~()

:contains
  |~~ Whether the subject holds the value: a string a substring, a
      vector or a set an element equal to it, the membership `has` asks.

      ~("hello" | contains "ell" | eq true)
      ~(["ops" "db"] | contains "ops" | eq true)
      ~(42 | contains "x" !| /addresses | eq #[::set/contains ::string/contains ::vec/contains])
  ~~|
  ::verb~()

:prepend
  |~~ The subject with the operand in front: a string takes a string as
      its prefix, a vector any value as its first element. The subject
      keeps its kind and the tags over it, a set staying a set through
      its constructor.

      ~("world" | prepend "hello " | eq "hello world")
      ~([2 3] | prepend 1 | eq [1 2 3])
      ~(42 | prepend "x" !| /addresses | eq #[::string/prepend ::vec/prepend])
  ~~|
  ::verb~()

:append
  |~~ The subject with the operand after it: a string takes a string as
      its suffix, a vector any value as its last element. The subject
      keeps its kind and the tags over it, a set staying a set through
      its constructor.

      ~("hello" | append " world" | eq "hello world")
      ~([1 2] | append 3 | eq [1 2 3])
      ~(42 | append "x" !| /addresses | eq #[::string/append ::vec/append])
  ~~|
  ::verb~()

:eq
  |~~ Whether the subject equals the value, by structural equality deep
      across maps, vectors, sets and scalars.

      ~(42 | eq 42 | eq true)
      ~({:a 1} | eq {:a 1} | eq true)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:value ::any | :returns ::boolean | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/eq})

:ne
  |~~ Whether the subject differs from the value, by the structural
      equality of `eq`, whose negation it is.

      ~(1 | ne 2 | eq true)
      ~({:a 1} | ne {:a 1} | eq false)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:value ::any | :returns ::boolean | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/ne})

:type
  |~~ The value's kind, a tag, which names the page of the value and the
      verbs it answers: `type | doc` reads the kind's page, and asking
      what a value is composes `type` with `eq`, `filter ~(type | eq
      ::string)`. A kind of the core is named under the prefix `qlang/`
      and written short.

      The literal of a value of the core implies its kind:
      ~(42 | type | eq ::number)
      ~("hi" | type | eq ::string)
      ~(:foo | type | eq ::keyword)
      ~([1 2] | type | eq ::vec)
      ~(#[1 2] | type | eq ::set)
      A verb, a binding record, a descriptor of the catalog, a quote and a
      tagged value answer the tag they carry:
      ~(::verb~(42) | type | eq ::verb)
      An error answers the tag of its site, which `!|` keeps on the
      descriptor it opens:
      ~(
        "hello" | add 1
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::AddLeftNotNumberError ::string]
       )
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:returns ::tag | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/type})

:json
  |~~ The subject rendered as a JSON string. The shapes of qlang alone
      lose their identity at this boundary: a keyword becomes its bare
      name, a key and a value alike (`:foo` → `"foo"`), a set flattens
      to an array, and a map writes its keys as the keys of an object.
      It hands values to consumers that do not read qlang.

      ~({:a 1 :b 2} | json | eq "{\"a\":1,\"b\":2}")
      ~([:foo :bar] | json | eq "[\"foo\",\"bar\"]")
      ~(#[1 2 3] | json | eq "[1,2,3]")
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:returns ::string | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/json})

:print
  |~~ The text that reads back as the subject, which the command line
      shows for an answer: an atom as its literal, a vector and a map by
      their brackets around the print of each part, the set, the quote,
      the doc and the error by their own brackets, and any other value
      under a tag as its tag before the print of its payload. A kind a
      module declares may answer `print` of its own, and the print of a
      value takes it for each part of that kind.

      ~(42 | print | eq "42")
      ~(#[2 1] | print | eq "#[1 2]")
      ~(::builtin{:a 1} | print | eq "::builtin{:a 1}")
      ~({:a [1 "x"]} | :v / | print | parse | :q / | apply q | eq v)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:returns ::string | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/print})

:elide
  |~~ The value within a budget of characters of its print, what does
      not fit given way to `::elision` markers: the largest part
      beneath the top gives way first, a vector keeping the first half of
      its elements and ending with a marker of the rest, a string or a map
      giving way whole. A marker's `:read` applied to the value `elide`
      took answers the part left out, and a value whose print fits is
      answered as it is. The command line applies it to every answer
      it prints when `--budget=N` asks it to.

      ~([1 2] | elide 100 | eq [1 2])
      ~({:s "a long line of text to leave out" :n 1} | elide 40 | /s | type | eq ::elision)
      ~({:s "a long line of text to leave out" :n 1} | :v / | elide 40 | /s/read | :r / | v | apply r | eq (v | /s))
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:budget ::number | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/elide})

:if
  |~~ The branch the condition chooses, run against the subject: `if
      cond ~(then) ~(else)`. The condition is a boolean computed at the
      call, each branch a quote, and only the chosen branch runs.

      ~(5 | if (gt 0) ~("positive") ~("non-positive") | eq "positive")
      ~(-1 | if (gt 0) ~("positive") ~("non-positive") | eq "non-positive")
      ~({:age 25} | if (/age | gte 18) ~("adult") ~("minor") | eq "adult")
      ~(5 | if (gt 0) ~(add 100) ~() | eq 105)

      A condition that is not a boolean is refused, since it has no
      second reading. The empty quote `~()` is the branch that
      leaves the subject as it is. ~~|
  ::verb~(:condition ::boolean | :then ::quote | :else ::quote | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/if})

::IfConditionNotBooleanError
  |~~ The condition of `if` answers a boolean; null, a number, a string
      or any other value is refused where it stands.
      ~(
        5 | if 1 ~("yes") ~("no")
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::IfConditionNotBooleanError ::number]
       )
  ~~|

::IfThenNotQuoteError
  |~~ The then branch of `if` is code it runs only when chosen, so it
      takes a quote, `if (gt 0) ~("yes") ~("no")`; every branch is checked at the call.
      ~(
        5 | if true "yes" ~("no")
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::IfThenNotQuoteError ::string]
       )
  ~~|

::IfElseNotQuoteError
  |~~ The else branch of `if` is code it runs only when chosen, so it
      takes a quote, `if (gt 0) ~("yes") ~("no")`; every branch is checked at the call.
      ~(
        5 | if true ~("yes") "no"
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::IfElseNotQuoteError ::string]
       )
  ~~|

:coalesce
  |~~ The first answer of the alternatives, run in order against the
      subject, that is neither null nor an error; every other value,
      false, 0, "", [], {} and #[] among them, ends the walk, and a walk
      that skips them all answers null. `coalesce ~(/key) ~(fallback)`
      reads "the key where the map holds it, else the fallback": `/key`
      answers an error on a map without the key, which the walk skips as
      it skips null. An alternative that is no quote answers itself, and a
      call of such alternatives alone puts the subject first, so
      `coalesce 30` reads "the subject, else 30".

      ~({:nickname null :name "alice"} | coalesce ~(/nickname) ~(/name) | eq "alice")
      ~({:nickname "ace" :name "alice"} | coalesce ~(/nickname) ~(/name) | eq "ace")
      ~({:a 2 :b 3} | coalesce ~(/missing) ~("fallback") | eq "fallback")
      ~({:nickname null :name null} | coalesce ~(/nickname) ~(/name) ~("anonymous") | eq "anonymous")
      ~([{:t 45} {:t null} {}] * (at :t | coalesce 30) | eq [45 30 30])
      ~({:tags ["a"]} | /tags | coalesce [] | eq ["a"])
      ~({:a 2} | coalesce ~(/missing) "fallback" | eq "fallback")
  ~~|
  ::verb~(* :alternatives #[::quote ::any] | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/coalesce})

::CoalesceNoAlternativesError
  |~~ `coalesce` answers the first of its alternatives that answers a
      value, so it takes one at least, `coalesce ~(/nickname) ~("anonymous")`.
      ~(
        {} | coalesce
        !| [type]
        |  eq [::CoalesceNoAlternativesError]
       )
  ~~|

:cond
  |~~ The branch of the first predicate that answers true against the
      subject, the clauses alternating predicate and branch, each
      predicate answering a boolean. A clause left over after the pairs
      is the default branch; without one, a walk that matches nothing
      answers null. It flattens a chain of nested `if`.

      ~(85 | cond ~(gte 90) ~("A") ~(gte 80) ~("B") ~(gte 70) ~("C") ~("F") | eq "B")
      ~(65 | cond ~(gte 90) ~("A") ~(gte 80) ~("B") ~(gte 70) ~("C") ~("F") | eq "F")
      ~({:role "admin"} | cond ~(/role | eq "admin") ~("full") ~(/role | eq "user") ~("limited") | eq "full")
  ~~|
  ::verb~(* :clauses ::quote | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/cond})

::CondNoBranchesError
  |~~ `cond` takes its clauses in pairs, a predicate and its branch, a
      last clause alone the default; a call without one pair, a lone
      default among them, has nothing to answer.
      ~(
        5 | cond
        !| [type]
        |  eq [::CondNoBranchesError]
       )
  ~~|

::CondConditionNotBooleanError
  |~~ Every predicate of `cond` answers a boolean; `:index` counts the
      modifiers from 1 and names the predicate that answered another
      kind.
      ~(
        5 | cond ~(1) ~("yes")
        !| [type /index /actualType]
        |  eq [::CondConditionNotBooleanError 1 ::number]
       )
  ~~|

:keyword
  |~~ The involution of strings and keywords: a string names its
      keyword, and a keyword answers its name, so `keyword | keyword`
      round-trips.

      ~("foo" | keyword | eq :foo)
      ~(:foo | keyword | eq "foo")
      ~(42 | keyword !| /addresses | eq #[::keyword/keyword ::string/keyword])
  ~~|
  ::verb~()

:parse
  |~~ The involution of source text and quotes, the way `keyword` flips
      a string and a keyword: text reads as the quote of its steps, and
      a quote prints back as its text.

      ~("add 1 2" | parse | eq ~(add 1 2))
      ~(~(add 1) | parse | eq "add 1")
      ~(42 | parse !| /addresses | eq #[::quote/parse ::string/parse])
  ~~|
  ::verb~()

:apply
  |~~ The value of the code run against the subject, under the fork
      rule: the declarations the code makes stay inside it, and only its
      value comes out. The subject comes first, as for every verb, so
      `x | apply /` runs a quote held as the subject against itself, and
      `"src" | parse | apply /` evaluates source text.

      The quote's first step rides `|` against the subject unless the
      quote carries a leading combinator (`*`, `!|`), which routes it
      through that combinator, so the pipeline suffix a `:trail` records
      runs again as it is.

      ~(42 | apply ~(type | eq ::null) | eq false)
      ~([1 2 3] | apply ~(* mul 2) | eq [2 4 6])
      ~([[1 2] [3 4]] | apply ~(flat | sort) | eq [1 2 3 4])
      ~("10 | add 3" | parse | apply / | eq 13)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:code ::quote | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/apply})

::ApplyCodeNotQuoteError
  |~~ The code `apply` runs on its subject is a quote, `apply ~(add 1)`,
      or a name that holds one; a value computed at the call is refused,
      the error a step answered there among them.

      ~(
        42 | apply 1
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::ApplyCodeNotQuoteError ::number]
       )
  ~~|

:env
  |~~ The names the scope holds where it stands, a map from each name
      to the record of its binding, `::binding` with its name, docs,
      value, source and module: what the query, the session and a
      module's `use` wrote, a fork's own declarations among them inside
      the fork. The verbs and the nouns of the core and of the hosts
      stay with their providers, listed from `::qlang | manifest`.
      `env | /flag` hands one record to the next step.

      ~(:limit 10 | env | keys | eq #[:limit])
      ~(env | has :count | not)
      ~(42 | :answer / | env | /answer | /value | eq 42)
      ~(99 | :secret / | env | has :secret | eq true)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:returns ::map | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/env})

:use
  |~~ The loader, the one verb that writes the scope of its call: it
      brings declarations into the names of a reader, where every other
      verb answers a value, and the value in the pipe goes on unchanged.
      Inside a fork the names it brings evaporate on close.

      A map brings its entries in, a record as the binding it is and any
      other value as a binding without a doc:
      ~({:limit 10 :role "admin"} | use | env | /limit | /value | eq 10)
      ~({:x 1} | use | x | add 2 | eq 3)
      A keyword loads the module it names, a vector several in order, a
      later one shadowing an earlier, and a set several refusing a name
      two of them export:
      ~(use :absolutely-nonexistent-namespace !| type | eq ::UseNamespaceNotFoundError)
      A second slot takes only the names it lists:
      ~(use :absolutely-nonexistent-namespace #[:a] !| type | eq ::UseNamespaceNotFoundError)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:namespace #[::keyword ::vec ::null] :names #[::keyword ::vec ::null] | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/use})

::UseSubjectNotMapError
  |~~ `use` alone brings the entries of a map into the scope, so its
      subject is a map; a module is loaded by its name, `use :ns`.

      ~(
        42 | use
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::UseSubjectNotMapError ::number]
       )
  ~~|

::UseNamespaceNotKeywordError
  |~~ `use :ns #[:name …]` takes the names of one namespace, so the
      namespace a selection reads from is a keyword.

      ~(
        use [:a] #[:x]
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::UseNamespaceNotKeywordError ::vec]
       )
  ~~|

::UseNamespaceNotFoundError
  |~~ `use :ns` found no module of the name: none was loaded under it
      and the host's locator answers none. A name the scope holds, a
      verb or a value, is no module, so `use :count` is refused too.

      ~(
        use :absolutely-nonexistent-namespace
        !| [type /namespaceName]
        |  eq [::UseNamespaceNotFoundError
                :absolutely-nonexistent-namespace]
       )
      ~(use :count !| type | eq ::UseNamespaceNotFoundError)
  ~~|

::UseNamespaceElementNotKeywordError
  |~~ `use [:a :b]` loads modules by their names, each a keyword;
      `:index` names the first that is not.

      ~(
        use [42 :foo]
        !| [type /index /actualType]
        |  eq [::UseNamespaceElementNotKeywordError 0 ::number]
       )
  ~~|

::UseNamespaceCollisionError
  |~~ `use #[:a :b]` loads modules that must not share a name, and two
      that export one are refused; a vector, `use [:a :b]`, lets the
      later win.
  ~~|

::UseNameNotExportedError
  |~~ `use :ns #[:name]` brings the named declarations of a module, and
      a name the module does not declare is refused.
  ~~|

::UseImplNamesNoVerbError
  |~~ A host handed an implementation for a name its module's source
      declares as no verb: the implementations a locator hands beside
      a source are the primitives of the verbs the source declares.
      `:namespaceName` names the module and `:implName` the name.
  ~~|

:binding
  |~~ The record of a name, what a declaration wrote into its scope:
      `::binding` with its `:name`, its `:docs`, its `:value`, its
      `:source` and the `:module` it came from, so where a name was
      declared is read as data. The other axes project it: `doc` its
      page, `source` its quote, `spec` its value. A keyword after it
      reads the record of the member the subject holds under it.

      A verb by its address reads the record its module declared:
      ~(::vec/filter | binding | /module | eq :qlang/vec)
      ~(::vec/filter | binding | type | eq ::binding)
      A name of the scope reads the record its declaration wrote:
      ~(:x 1 | :x | binding | /value | eq 1)
      Any other value reads the record of its kind:
      ~(5 | binding | /name | eq ::number)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:anchor #[::keyword ::null] | :returns ::binding | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/binding})

::BindingNotFoundError
  |~~ `binding` — no declaration found for the name it read: a keyword
      that names no binding of its scope, the name of a verb a provider
      keeps among them, or a tag name that names nothing. `:addresses`
      holds the addresses of the pages of that name, the contract on any
      value among them, whose page holds for every verb of the name.

      ~(
        :nonexistent-binding | binding
        !| [type /bindingName]
        |  eq [::BindingNotFoundError :nonexistent-binding]
       )
  ~~|

:source
  |~~ The code of the declaration that binds a name in its scope, the
      `:source` of its record, which `parse` prints as its text; `::Tag | source` the tag's. A binding no
      step declared, a value `use` or a host bound, answers null.
      Any other value reads the declaration of its kind. Given a name,
      it reads the member the subject holds under it: the slot a
      verb's head declares, and for any other subject the verb the name
      calls after it.

      ~(::vec/filter | source | parse | contains ":filter")
      ~(::AddLeftNotNumberError | source | parse | contains "AddLeftNotNumberError")
      ~("text" | source | eq (::string | source))
      ~(::vec/take | source :count | parse | eq ":count ::number")
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:name #[::keyword ::null] | :returns #[::quote ::null] | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/source})

::SourceBindingNotFoundError
  |~~ `source` — no declaration found for the name it read: a keyword
      that names no binding of its scope, the name of a verb a provider
      keeps among them, or a tag name that names nothing. `:addresses`
      holds the addresses of the pages of that name, the contract on any
      value among them, whose page holds for every verb of the name.

      ~(
        :nonexistent-binding | source
        !| [type /bindingName]
        |  eq [::SourceBindingNotFoundError :nonexistent-binding]
       )
      ~(:filter | source !| /addresses | has ::vec/filter)
  ~~|

:doc
  |~~ The page of a name, the passage from an address into its
      description: `::vec/filter | doc` the page of a verb by its
      address, `:name | doc` the page of the declaration that binds
      `:name` in its scope, null for a declaration without one. A page
      answers its text by `content`, its laws by `laws`, its links by
      `links` and its snippets by `snippets`. A keyword after `doc` is an
      anchor within the page of its subject, a vector of anchors a path,
      each read within the page the one before it reached, and a set of
      anchors several pages in one query.

      A name reads its own page:
      ~(::vec/filter | doc | content | contains "predicate")
      ~(::AddLeftNotNumberError | doc | type | eq ::doc)
      Any other value reads the page of its kind, and the `:kind` field
      of a map names nothing:
      ~(5 | doc | eq (::number | doc))
      ~({:kind ::set} | doc | eq (::map | doc))
      A keyword reads the page of the member the subject holds under it,
      the verb it calls or the slot a verb's head declares:
      ~(::map | doc :minus | eq (::map/minus | doc))
      ~([1 2] | doc :count | eq (::vec/count | doc))
      ~(:f ::verb~(:n |~~ how many ~~| ::number | add n) | :f | doc :n | content | eq " how many ")
      A vector of anchors walks a path, and a set of anchors reads a page
      at each:
      ~(::vec | doc [:take] | eq (::vec/take | doc))
      ~(::qlang | doc #[:pipeline :errors] | count | eq 2)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:anchor #[::keyword ::vec ::set ::null] | :returns #[::doc ::null ::vec] | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/doc})

::DocBindingNotFoundError
  |~~ `doc` — no declaration found for the name it read: a keyword that
      names no binding of its scope, the name of a verb a provider keeps
      among them, a tag name that names nothing, or an anchor that names
      no member of the page before it. `:addresses` holds the addresses
      of the pages of that name, the contract on any value among them,
      whose page holds for every verb of the name.

      ~(
        :nonexistent-binding | doc
        !| [type /bindingName]
        |  eq [::DocBindingNotFoundError :nonexistent-binding]
       )
      ~(:filter | doc !| /addresses | has ::vec/filter)
      ~(:reduce | doc !| /addresses | first | doc | eq (::qlang/any/reduce | doc))
  ~~|

::DocAnchorNotKeywordError
  |~~ `doc` — an anchor of a path is no keyword: each step of the path
      names a member by a keyword.

      ~(::vec | doc [:take 1] !| [type /index] | eq [::DocAnchorNotKeywordError 1])
  ~~|

:explain
  |~~ The explanation of the value, `::explanation{:page … :value …}`:
      the page `doc` reads for it, then the value itself, so it prints
      as the page above what it explains. A name reads its own
      page, a value the page of the kind it shows, and an error `!|`
      opened the page of its site, which says what failed.

      ~(42 | explain | page | eq (::number | doc))
      ~(::number/add | explain | page | content | contains "Adds two numbers")
      ~("x" | add 1 !| explain | page | content | contains "adds numbers")
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:returns ::explanation | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/explain})

::ExplainBindingNotFoundError
  |~~ `explain` — no declaration found for the page of its subject: a
      keyword that names no binding of its scope, or a tag name that
      names nothing.

      ~(
        :nonexistent-binding | explain
        !| [type /bindingName]
        |  eq [::ExplainBindingNotFoundError :nonexistent-binding]
       )
  ~~|

:spec
  |~~ The declaration a name reads, what a reader checks before a call:
      what a verb takes and answers, what a refusal knows of its site,
      what a kind is made of. A projection of a signature reads the body
      of the declaration it names; the loader's `use` answers its
      descriptor, any other binding the value its record holds, and any
      other value the declaration of its kind.

      A verb answers its signature, a `::spec~(…)` whose subject is its
      noun and whose `:throws` lists its refusals:
      ~(::number/add | spec | /subject | eq ::number)
      ~(:twice ::verb~(:x ::number | mul 2) | :twice | spec | type | eq ::spec)
      A refusal answers the facts of its site, `:category`, `:operand`,
      `:position` and `:expectedType`, and an error the facts of its tag:
      ~(::AddLeftNotNumberError | spec | /operand | eq :add)
      ~("x" | add 1 !| spec | /category | eq :typeError)
      A kind answers its constructor, `:impl`, its refusals and its verbs:
      ~(::verb | spec | /impl | eq :qlang/type/verb)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:name #[::keyword ::null] | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/spec})

::SpecBindingNotFoundError
  |~~ `spec` — no declaration found for the name it read: a keyword that
      names no binding of its scope, the name of a verb a provider keeps
      among them, or a tag name that names nothing. `:addresses` holds
      the addresses of the pages of that name, the contract on any value
      among them, whose page holds for every verb of the name.

      ~(
        :nonexistent-binding | spec
        !| [type /bindingName]
        |  eq [::SpecBindingNotFoundError :nonexistent-binding]
       )
      ~(:filter | spec !| /addresses | has ::vec/filter)
  ~~|

:runLaws
  |~~ Walks every quote among the segments of the docs of the binding a
      name reads, a keyword or a tag name, the address of a verb among
      them, and answers a vector of result maps shaped `{:snippet
      :actual :ok :error}`. Each quote runs in an isolated scope; `:ok`
      is true iff the example answers true, every other answer, an
      error value among them, failing it. The composition `::number |
      spec | /verbs | every ~(runLaws | every ~(/ok))` runs every
      example of the verbs of numbers and must answer true.

      ~(::number/add | runLaws * /ok | distinct | eq #[true])
      ~(::number/gt | runLaws | count | gte 1)
      ~(42 | runLaws !| /addresses | eq #[::keyword/runLaws ::tag/runLaws])
  ~~|
  ::verb~()

::RunLawsBindingNotFoundError
  |~~ `runLaws` — no declaration found for the name it read, as `doc`
      finds none: a keyword that names no binding of its scope, the name
      of a verb a provider keeps among them, or a tag name that names
      nothing. `:addresses` holds the addresses of the pages of that
      name, the contract on any value among them, whose page holds for
      every verb of the name.

      ~(
        :nonexistent-binding | runLaws
        !| [type /bindingName]
        |  eq [::RunLawsBindingNotFoundError :nonexistent-binding]
       )
      ~(:filter | runLaws !| /addresses | has ::vec/filter)
      ~(::Nonexistent | runLaws !| /bindingName | eq ::Nonexistent)
  ~~|

:tag
  |~~ Lays a tag over a value, the name of the kind it is read as, the
      partner of the split `[type payload]`: a value is minted under a
      tag at run time as a literal `::Tag{…}` mints it where it is
      written. `value | tag ::Foo` tags its subject, `[tag value] | tag`
      reads the pair `[type payload]` answers, and `tag value tag` takes
      both, so `pair | tag /1 /0` reorders a pair into its place.

      A tag stands over the value, which its payload answers:
      ~(42 | tag ::Box | payload | eq 42)
      ~([1 2 3] | tag ::Triple | type | eq ::Triple)
      A tagged value split into its type and payload tags back into
      itself:
      ~(::Box {} | ::Box[1 2 3] | [type payload] | tag | eq ::Box[1 2 3])
      A tag over a tagged value nests it; replacing the tag goes through
      the payload, `tagged | payload | tag ::Other`:
      ~(::Inner {} | ::Outer {} | ::Inner[1] | tag ::Outer | payload | type | eq ::Inner)
      A tag laid over an error answers the error unchanged, since no tag
      stands over one; the fail track opens an error to its descriptor,
      which a tag and `raise` rename:
      ~([::Box (!{:k 1})] | tag !| type | eq ::error)
      ~(1 | add "1" !| [type payload] | tag | raise !| type | eq ::AddRightNotNumberError)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:name #[::tag ::null] | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/tag})

::TagModifierNotTagKeywordError
  |~~ The tag `tag` lays is a tag name, `tag ::Box`; a keyword, `:box`,
      names a field and no kind.
      ~(
        42 | tag :foo
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::TagModifierNotTagKeywordError ::keyword]
       )
  ~~|

::TagBareSubjectShapeError
  |~~ `tag` alone reads a pair, `[::Box value] | tag`, the tag and the
      value it goes over; a subject of another shape is refused, and
      `:actualLength` counts a vector of another length.
      ~(
        42 | tag
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::TagBareSubjectShapeError ::number]
       )
      ~(
        [::Foo 1 2] | tag
        !| [type /actualLength]
        |  eq [::TagBareSubjectShapeError 3]
       )
  ~~|
